# CLOSED — polymorphic functions now specialize at call sites.
#
# This file is kept as the regression it became. `kex -C` REJECTS it now:
#
#   error: Cannot add Integer and String
#
# What the audit found, and what it was:
#
# `id(x) = x` was accepted at both `Int` and `String` (good), but the result
# was not constrained: `n` was not typed as `Int`, `s` was not typed as
# `String`. The checker accepted `n + s` and the program crashed at runtime.
# Direct `5 + "a"` WAS rejected, so the bug was specifically that `id(5)` did
# not produce an `Int`-typed result — free type variables from a function body
# never got bound to the argument type at the call site.
#
# The cause: an unannotated parameter is an inference variable living in one
# global substitution, so every call site shared it. An ANNOTATED generic
# (`let first(xs: [A]) -> A?`) was already a per-signature placeholder that
# call sites instantiate afresh; `TypeChecker::generalizeSignature` now moves
# a definition's leftover inference variables into that same representation,
# so inferred and annotated generics behave identically from there on.
#
# Haskell (HM + let-polymorphism), which Kex now matches here:
#   id :: a -> a
#   n  = id 5      -- inferred n :: Int
#   s  = id "a"    -- inferred s :: [Char], separate instantiation
#   n + s          -- COMPILE ERROR: cannot match expected type Int with [Char]
#
# It had also grown a HANG — `kex -C`, the LSP, and every other mode stopped
# responding on this file — from a separate defect: `n + s` resolved both
# operands to one shared variable, which was then bound to itself, and
# `TypeChecker::resolve` walks the substitution chain to a fixed point that no
# longer existed. Fixed too; see `spec/self_referential_typevar.kex`.
#
# Specs: spec/error_polymorphic_instantiation.kex (this program's diagnostics)
#        spec/polymorphic_reuse.kex (the calls that must keep working)

let id(x) = x

main do
  let n = id(5)
  let s = id("a")
  IO.printLine(n + s)
end
